 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Women in Victoria are in work in similar numbers to a year ago, but their unemployment rate has risen noticeably over the past year and is now slightly above men's.\n\n**Latest (September 2026, seasonally adjusted, Victoria):**\n- **Unemployment rate: 5.1%**, up from 4.1% in September 2025 and about 4.0% in late 2025. It was at its low of about 3.9% in April 2025.\n- **Men's unemployment rate: 5.0%.** For most of 2025 women's rate was below men's (September 2025: women 4.1%, men 5.3%). It has now converged.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio: 60.4%**, close to the 61.3% peak in late 2025.\n- **Participation rate: 63.6%**, compared with 71.5% for men. It has been fairly flat, around 63–64%, over the past two years.\n- **Full-time employment: about 1,008,000 women**, up roughly 39,000 from October 2024 but down from about 1,037,000 in July 2025.\n\n**What's changed:** Women's unemployment rose steadily from about 4.2% in February 2026 to a peak of 5.2% in July 2026. It has eased slightly since, to 5.1% in September. Men's rate rose over the same period, so this is a broader softening in Victoria's labour market rather than something specific to women, though the gap has closed.\n\nMonthly figures are noisy, so a single month's move shouldn't be over-read. The trend over the past year is the more reliable signal.",
